# Started learning Zig!

Why? Why a new language in the day and age of LLMs — are you mad? Let AI do the work.

**Because I want to.**

The first real programming language I learned was C in my college days. Programming was hard, harder than I had expected it to be. I did a lot of HTML and CSS before college, but never a real programming language. Not even Python/PHP etc. And starting with C made it that much harder. Over the course of my semester while learning C, every problem I solved gave me a sense of confidence — which was broken the next problem I solved. But at the end of it all that endurance, it made me understand computers, on what goes in it.

## The Java years

After the first semester, we moved to Java, and man was it frustrating. Why do we need to learn a language when we barely learnt C? I became a rebel — asked professors why Java, what does it bring to the table, why do we need to learn it over C — and none could answer this curiosity of mine.

Ultimately we moved on from Java to the web technologies of the time: **the LAMP stack**, Python, Perl, etc.

## Python, and a lingering question

I joined the industry after my degree was over. I had learnt Django in one of my internships, and Django was all the rage in our startup ecosystem — hence Python was *THE* language that I ended up using in the industry, and continue to use today even after 10 years of being in the industry.

But there's a catch — ah, there's always a catch. In these 10 years I never did have to use the C language. My infatuation and personal experiments never stopped on finding the answer to my question: **why not C?**

I came across a lot of answers, and every one of them sounded really solid and reasonable to me on why the industry did not prefer C:

- Memory unsafe.
- Manual memory management reduces productivity.
- Large codebases are hard to maintain with C.
- Implicit behaviours, undefined behaviours.
- etc.

These reasons sounded so perfect on why not C that whenever I tried learning C, I always found it hard to get started and could resonate with them. But — yeah, there's a but again. I always wondered: C being such a *simple* language, why do we need complex languages? And why were there no major efforts to improve on C? Yeah, there were a lot of efforts over the years, but nothing panned out really — what we got instead was C++, Java, and with it all the complexity.

C lacked all the modern features that we take for granted in languages like Python, which make development faster and easier:

- Package management.
- Modern tooling.
- etc.

## So, why Zig?

Somehow I stumbled upon Zig — *a better C*. Not just better, but a better alternative. From what it looks like at the moment anyways; I have only written basic programs in Zig, so what do I know.

BUT why Zig? Why not just use LLMs, C3, or any other language?

**Because I want to.**

Anyways, these were just a bunch of thoughts I had while I was trying to learn Zig.
